This commit was written three times, and the drafts are more
interesting than the result.

The first argued the change on turn-cost — every commit is a two-turn
negotiation. That is a real effect but the wrong reason. The reason is
that the message records a perceived motivation, and an agent that
cannot state one has probably misunderstood the change; cost is the
symptom, the diagnostic value is the point. Worth knowing that the
weaker framing is what comes out first if nobody pushes.

The second kept notes as a proposal, shown but not attached until the
user agreed. I even flagged in that draft's own notes that a distracted
user would end up with commits carrying no notes at all. Flagging a
problem is not fixing it: the gate was the same stall the commit
removes, moved one object further down, and the same argument that
makes an unprompted commit safe — corrections are cheap — applies
verbatim to `git notes edit`. Notes now always get saved.

The test in step 3 is self-graded, and that is its weak point. Asked
"can you state why this change was made?", a model can nearly always
construct something plausible from the diff. This commit is the
example: I judged the motivation clear because I had written the plan
that supplied it, then reproduced my own reasoning and called it
recovered. The three conditions are written to fire on structural
signals rather than felt confidence, but I have not measured how often
they fire.

One gap I noticed and did not close: none of the three conditions asks
why the change is being made *now*. Peff messages often carry that
("not urgent, but since we're here"), and neither of these two commits
records what prompted the work. A fourth condition for it is tempting,
but it risks firing on nearly every commit and dragging the default
back toward always-interviewing, which is what this commit sets out to
stop.
